**: No compilation error Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected premium 65, received premium 80 Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase.\n\nGreen: implement component block logic. A block of exactly 3 alike components costs 60. So for components, group by type, count. For each type: full blocks of 3 -> 60 each, remainder -> 25 each.\n\nMinimal implementation: since the active test only has 3 runes, I could hardcode: if there are exactly 3 runes, return 65. But that's not clean. Better to implement the grouping logic.\n\nActually, given the upcoming tests (4 runes, 7 runes, mixed runes/moonstones), implementing the full block logic now is appropriate and minimal in the sense of natural next step. The skill says \"Generalization (Only When Forced)\". But the current test forces generalization because we need to handle 3 alike components.\n\nLet me implement component block logic:\n- Separate items into main items and components.\n- Components grouped by type.\n- For each component type: blocks = floor(count / 3) but only if count === 3? Actually block requires exactly 3. \"block requires exactly 3\" - 4 runes -> 100 (no block). So a block applies only to groups of exactly 3. For 7 runes: no block, 7*25=175.\n  \nWait, re-read: \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" Examples:\n- 2 runes -> 50\n- 3 runes -> 60\n- 4 runes -> 100 (no block — block requires exactly 3)\n- 7 runes -> 175\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones -> 120 (two separate blocks)\n\nSo a block applies when count is exactly 3. If count is 6, is it two blocks? The example doesn't show 6. But 7 runes -> 175 = 7*25, not 2*60 + 25. So blocks only apply to exactly 3, not multiples. Interesting.\n\nWait, 6 runes would presumably be two blocks? But the rule says \"block requires exactly 3\". Actually the 7 runes example says no block — block requires exactly 3. That implies 6 runes might be two blocks of 3? Or no blocks at all? The wording is ambiguous.\n\nLet me re-read: \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" And example: \"7 runes → 175 G base premium\". If blocks could stack as groups of 3, 7 would be 2 blocks + 1 = 145. But it's 175, so no block discount at all. So the rule is: if the count of a component type is exactly 3, use 60; otherwise use count * 25.\n\nBut then 3 runes + 3 moonstones = two blocks = 120. That's two separate types, each exactly 3.\n\nSo the logic is: for each component type, if count === 3 -> 60, else -> count * 25.\n\nThis is the minimal rule based on examples. I'll implement that.\n\nGreen implementation: add component handling in basePremiumFor or in sumBasePremiums. Since we need to count components, sumBasePremiums should group items by type first.\n\nBut for minimal change, I can implement a function that calculates component block premium.\n\nLet me modify sumBasePremiums to handle components. Actually, I can create a function that takes all items and returns the total base premium with block logic.\n\nCurrent structure:\n- basePremiumFor returns per-item base.\n- sumBasePremiums sums them.\n\nFor blocks, we need to look at the whole list. So I should change the approach: compute base premium for the whole item list at once, handling component blocks.\n\nLet me write:\n```typescript\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM_GOLD = 60;\n\nconst basePremiumForItems = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const componentCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let mainItemPremium = 0;\n  \n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (item.type === \"rune\" || item.type === \"moonstone\") {\n      componentCounts.set(item.type, (componentCounts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n    } else {\n      mainItemPremium += basePremiumFor(item);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  let componentPremium = 0;\n  for (const count of componentCounts.values()) {\n    if (count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n      componentPremium += COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM_GOLD;\n    } else {\n      componentPremium += count * BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD.rune;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return mainItemPremium + componentPremium;\n};\n```\n\nThis is reasonable. But green phase says minimal. The active test only has 3 runes. I could hardcode for now. But that creates more churn. I think implementing the block logic is appropriate as it's the natural generalization forced by the test.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The current `basePremiumFor` and `sumBasePremiums` structure doesn't work for blocks because blocks depend on the group. The test forces us to change the architecture. So implementing the grouping is the minimal change that makes the test pass.\n\nLet me do it.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.write:67","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-37-06_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7/src/claim-office.ts","content":"const PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD = 5;\nconst UNKNOWN_TYPE_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD = 100;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM_GOLD = 60;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n\ntype ScenarioInput = {\n  steps: Array<{ items?: unknown[] }>;\n};\n\ntype Item = {\n  type?: string;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (item: Item): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD[item.type ?? \"\"] ?? UNKNOWN_TYPE_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD;\n\nconst basePremiumForItems = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const componentCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let mainItemPremium = 0;\n\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const type = item.type ?? \"\";\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n      componentCounts.set(type, (componentCounts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n    } else {\n      mainItemPremium += basePremiumFor(item);\n    }\n  }\n\n  let componentPremium = 0;\n  for (const count of componentCounts.values()) {\n    if (count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n      componentPremium += COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM_GOLD;\n    } else {\n      componentPremium += count * BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD.rune;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return mainItemPremium + componentPremium;\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const items = (scenario as ScenarioInput).steps[0].items ?? [];\n  const basePremium = basePremiumForItems(items as Item[]);\n  return { results: [{ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD }] };\n};\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":616,"output":1823,"cacheRead":42112,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1258,"totalTokens":44551,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784923206510,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-71a93e37-79ca-42c8-811f-16c801ef32ce","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.write:67","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 1556 bytes to /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-37-06_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7/src/claim-office.ts"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784923216629},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:68","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":80,"output":19,"cacheRead":44544,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":44643,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784923216630,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-41381e97-e6ae-4147-89f4-4767540d0e34","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:68","toolName":"bash","content":