ration  161ms (transform 31ms, setup 0ms, collect 30ms, tests 9ms, environment 0ms, prepare 41ms)\n\n ELIFECYCLE  Test failed. See above for more details.\n\n\nCommand exited with code 1"}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp":1784923453917},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"## Red -- Test 13: should add 50% curse surcharge to a cursed sword -- premium 155 G\n\nRed Phase - Compilation Error Prediction:\n- Expected: No compilation error\n- Actual: No compilation error\n\nRed Phase - Runtime Error Prediction:\n- Expected: Assertion error, premium 105 vs 155\n- Reason: Cursed modifier not yet applied\n- Actual: Assertion error as predicted\n\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Compilation Prediction**: No compilation error Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected premium 155, received premium 105 Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase.\n\nGreen: implement curse surcharge. For a cursed item, add 50% of base premium. Current code returns basePremiumForItem only. I need to compute modified base premium per item.\n\nFor minimal change, I'll update totalBasePremiumFor to account for item modifiers. Actually, for the active test, only one cursed sword. Let me modify basePremiumForItem to accept item and apply curse.\n\nWait, basePremiumForItem returns the base premium. The modifiers apply to the base premium. I need a different concept: item premium after item-specific modifiers.\n\nLet me restructure:\n- `basePremiumForItem(item)`: base premium (100 for sword)\n- `itemPremiumAfterModifiers(item)`: base + cursed surcharge + high enchantment surcharge\n- `totalBasePremiumFor` becomes `totalItemPremiumFor` or similar.\n\nBut for minimal change to pass current test, I can update the code so that when summing, it applies curse modifier.\n\nActually, the architecture matters because policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base premium (sum of item base premiums), while item-specific modifiers apply per item. So I need:\n1. Sum of item base premiums (policy base)\n2. Item-specific surcharges\n3. Policy-wide modifiers applied to (policy base + item-specific surcharges)? Or to policy base only?\n\nRe-read spec: \"item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) apply to the base premium of the affected item; policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium (the sum of all item base premiums); the processing fee is added at the very end\"\n\nSo:\n- For each item, compute item-specific modified premium: base * (1 + curse surcharge + high enchantment surcharge)\n- Policy base premium = sum of item base premiums (unmodified)\n- Apply policy-wide modifiers to policy base premium\n- Add item-specific surcharges? Wait, the example: cursed sword (base 100) + plain amulet (base 60). Policy base = 160. Cursed surcharge = 50% of cursed sword's base = 50. So total before policy-wide = 160 + 50 = 210.\n\nActually the example says: \"policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G ... → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\"\n\nSo total = policy base premium (sum of unmodified base) + item-specific surcharges + policy-wide modifiers + fee.\n\nLet me compute: policy base = 160, cursed surcharge = 50% * 100 = 50. Subtotal = 210. No policy-wide modifiers.\n\nFor the integration example: newcomer with cursed sword (steel, enchantment 3) -> 100 base + 50 curse + 10 first insurance = 160. Wait, first insurance is policy-wide (10% of policy base). Policy base = 100. First insurance = 10. So subtotal = 100 + 50 + 10 = 160. Plus fee 5 = 165. Matches.\n\nFor long-standing customer's second contract: cursed sword enchantment 7. 100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment - 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance - 15 follow-up = 155 + 5 = 160.\n\nPolicy base = 100. Item-specific surcharges = 50 + 30 = 80. Policy-wide modifiers: loyalty = -20% of 100 = -20, first insurance = +10% of 100 = +10, follow-up = -15% of 100 = -15. Total = 100 + 80 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155. Plus fee 5 = 160.\n\nSo the formula is:\nfinal = policyBase + itemSurcharges + policyWideModifiers + fee\n\nWhere:\n- policyBase = sum of item base premiums\n- itemSurcharges = sum over items of base_i * (curseRate + enchantRate)\n- policyWideModifiers = policyBase * (loyaltyRate + firstInsuranceRate + followUpRate)\n\nActually, looking at the first insurance: the spec says \"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\" And in the integration example, even though it's the second contract, each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance. Wait, that's confusing. Let me re-read.\n\n\"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\" - follow-up contract discount.\n\"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\" - first insurance surcharge.\n\nIntegration example: \"customer: 3 years with MHPCO; this is the customer's second quote in the scenario. item: a cursed sword. premium: 160. (100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment − 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance − 15 follow-up contract = 155 G + 5 G fee = 160 G). The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nSo both first insurance and follow-up contract apply? That seems contradictory. The example explicitly includes both +10 and -15. So the rule is: first insurance surcharge applies per quote item, and follow-up contract discount applies because this is not the first quote. Both apply to policy base.\n\nOK, so all policy-wide modifiers can apply simultaneously. The rates are:\n- loyalty: -20% if years >= 2\n- first insurance: +10% (always? per item/quote?)\n- follow-up: -15% if not first quote\n\nBut how do we know if it's the first quote or follow-up? The scenario has steps array. If a quote is not the first step? Actually \"this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\" - meaning there were two quote steps before? Or it's the second contract with MHPCO?\n\nWait, the input is a single scenario with a customer and steps. The follow-up contract discount applies to \"each contract after their first\". How do we determine first vs follow-up? It must be based on the position in the scenario: the first quote step is the first contract, subsequent quote steps are follow-ups. But the example says \"this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\".\n\nSo we need to track step index and apply follow-up discount to all quotes except the first.\n\nBut wait, the prompt says \"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\" This is customer history, not scenario position. But the integration example clarifies: \"this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\". So in the scenario, the second quote gets follow-up discount.\n\nThis is an important detail. Let me think about implementation later.\n\nFor now, the active test is just cursed sword with no customer modifiers. I need to add curse surcharge.\n\nMinimal implementation:\n- Add item-specific surcharge calculation.\n- For cursed items, add 50% of base.\n\nLet me implement.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:82","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-37-06_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7/src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":615,"output":1649,"cacheRead":49280,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1585,"totalTokens":51544,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784923453919,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c3597f6e-887e-4046-a3ba-37e4ffd49d65","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:82","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"const PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD = 5;\nconst UNKNOWN_ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD = 100;\nconst COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK = 3;\nconst BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM_GOLD = 25;\n\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM_GOLD,\n  moonstone: COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM_GOLD,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\n\ntype ScenarioInput = {\n  steps: Array<{ items?: unknown[] }>;\n};\n\ntype Item = {\n  type?: string;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD[item.type ?? \"\"] ?? UNKNOWN_ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD;\n\nconst totalBasePremiumFor = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const componentCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let mainItemPremium = 0;\n\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const type = item.type ?? \"\";\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n      componentCounts.set(type, (componentCounts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n    } else {\n      mainItemPremium += basePremiumForItem(item);\n    }\n  }\n\n  let componentPremium = 0;\n  for (const count of componentCounts.values()) {\n    componentPremium += componentPremiumForCount(count);\n  }\n\n  return mainItemPremium + componentPremium;\n};\n\nconst componentPremiumForCount = (count: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK\n    ? BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD\n    : count * COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM_GOLD;\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const items = (scenario as ScenarioInput).steps[0].items ?? [];\n  const basePremium = totalBasePremiumFor(items as Item[]);\n  return { results: [{ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD }] };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784923464222},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to apply curse surcharge. Currently totalBasePremiumFor returns the policy base (sum of unmodified base premiums). I need to also compute item-specific surcharges.\n\nLet me update the code. I'll compute policy base and item surcharges separately.\n\nFor components, curse/high enchantment probably doesn't apply (spec examples don't mention cursed runes). The cursed surcharge applies to main items. Let me apply item modifiers only to non-component items for now.\n\nActually, the Item type needs cursed and enchantment fields.\n\nLet me update:\n```typescript\ntype Item = {\n  type?: string;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  material?: string;\n};\n```\n\nFor totalBasePremiumFor, return an object { policyBase, itemSurcharges }. Or compute total premium before policy-wide modifiers and fee.\n\nActually for minimal change to pass the active test, I can just apply curse surcharge within totalBasePremiumFor. But the architecture needs to distinguish policy base and item surcharges because policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base.\n\nFor the current test (only cursed sword), no policy-wide modifiers. So I can just add curse surcharge to the total.\n\nBut upcoming tests will need the separation. Let me implement it properly now since the active test forces item-specific modifier logic.\n\nI'll compute:\n- policyBase = sum of unmodified item base premiums\n- itemSurcharges = sum of (base * modifier rates)\n\nThen processScenario computes final premium = policyBase + itemSurcharges + policyWideModifiers + fee.\n\nBut for now policyWideModifiers = 0.\n\nLet me write:\n```typescript\ntype Item = {\n  type?: string;\n  cursed?: boolean;\n  enchantment?: number;\n  material?: string;\n};\n\nconst itemSurchargeFor = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += base * 0.5;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= 5) surcharge += base * 0.3;\n  return surcharge;\n};\n\ntype PremiumBreakdown = {\n  policyBase: number;\n  itemSurcharges: number;\n};\n\nconst premiumBreakdownForItems = (items: Item[]): PremiumBreakdown => {\n  cons