n has two loops with mutation; extracting functions could improve intent.\n\nLet's explore a more elegant implementation:\n\n```\nconst premiumBreakdownForItems = (items: Item[]): PremiumBreakdown => {\n  const componentCounts = countComponents(items);\n  const { policyBase, itemSurcharges } = nonComponentBreakdown(items);\n  const componentPremium = componentPremiumForCounts(componentCounts);\n  return { policyBase: policyBase + componentPremium, itemSurcharges };\n};\n\nconst countComponents = (items: Item[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const type = item.type ?? \"\";\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n      counts.set(type, (counts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n\nconst nonComponentBreakdown = (items: Item[]): PremiumBreakdown => {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const type = item.type ?? \"\";\n    if (!COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n      const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n      policyBase += base;\n      itemSurcharges += curseSurchargeForItem(item, base); // pass base to avoid recomputing\n    }\n  }\n  return { policyBase, itemSurcharges };\n};\n```\n\nBut this iterates items twice (two loops) and checks component types twice. Tests still pass. It improves separation but adds functions. Might not reduce mass. Also `curseSurchargeForItem` could accept base to avoid recomputing base. Currently `itemSurchargeFor` recomputes base; we can optimize. But not necessary.\n\nMaybe we should keep it simple and minimal. The requirement: MUST attempt at least one refactoring. We can do small, safe refactor and document mass.\n\nHowever, the instruction says \"Apply Simple Design Rules in priority order\". We should evaluate each rule. We need produce final report with before/after mass and tests. We can do one or two refactorings. But must ensure tests green. Need maybe run linter too? Not required but good.\n\nLet's run `pnpm test` after edits. Use edit tool. First decide exact changes.\n\nRefactoring plan:\n- Rename `itemSurchargeFor` to `curseSurchargeForItem` (Rule 2 naming). Also update call site.\n- Remove `rune` and `moonstone` entries from `BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD` (Rule 3/4). They duplicate `COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM_GOLD` and are unused.\n- Move `componentPremiumForCount` above `premiumBreakdownForItems` (Rule 2 ordering).\n- Maybe update `componentPremiumForCount` ternary to use multiplication in both branches? Current uses block base if count===3 else count*unit. That is clear. Keep.\n- Maybe improve `premiumBreakdownForItems` naming of variables? `componentPremium` could become `componentBasePremium`. Not necessary.\n\nLet's also consider adding a small helper for `componentPremiumForCounts` to reduce duplication in loop? Current loop:\n```\nlet componentPremium = 0;\nfor (const count of componentCounts.values()) {\n  componentPremium += componentPremiumForCount(count);\n}\npolicyBase += componentPremium;\n```\nWe could compute directly:\n```\nconst componentPremium = Array.from(componentCounts.values()).reduce((sum, count) => sum + componentPremiumForCount(count), 0);\npolicyBase += componentPremium;\n```\nThis eliminates `let` and `+=` inside loop (one assignment for `componentPremium` const binding). But adds `Array.from` invocation, `reduce` invocation, arrow function bindings. Mass may not reduce much, clarity maybe worse. So skip.\n\nAlternative refactor: use for-of loop with accumulation is fine.\n\nMaybe we can improve `processScenario` by extracting the premium calculation to a named constant `premium` to reveal the formula. Currently it returns object with expression. We can do:\n```\nconst premium = policyBase + itemSurcharges + PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD;\nreturn { results: [{ premium }] };\n```\nThis adds a binding but no mass change maybe, improves readability.\n\nCould also extract `firstStepItems` to avoid casting. But maybe later tests will have multiple steps/claims. Let's keep minimal.\n\nLet's compute mass before and after. We need produce counts. APP mass can be based on final code. Need decide counting method. We'll be explicit. Count only executable code statements in implementation, ignoring type declarations. But we need count constants, bindings, invocations, conditionals, loops, assignments.\n\nLet's define counting rules to be consistent:\n- Constant: numeric/string literal (including `0`, `\"\"`, array literal `[]`? The instruction says constants: literal values (`5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`). Array/object literals not constants? But object property value literals count. We'll count string/numeric/boolean literals and `[]` maybe not.\n- Binding: variable/parameter declarations (const/let/function parameter). Destructured identifiers count each. Exported names count.\n- Invocation: function/method calls and operators `+`, `*`, `===`, `??`? The instruction says invocations: Function calls (`calculate()`, `Math.max()`). Binary operators like `+` are not function calls, but in the example they counted `reduce` and `+` as invocations: \"Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4\". So they count arithmetic operators as invocations. Also property access? They counted `numbers.reduce` as invocation. They counted `+` as invocation. They may also count method call `.has`. We'll count all function/method calls and arithmetic/comparison operators (`+`, `*`, `===`). Not property accesses or `??`. `??` maybe conditional? We can ignore or count as conditional. In example they didn't count `??`. The `reduce` example: `numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);` counted constants 1 (0), bindings 3 (numbers, sum, num), invocations 2 (reduce, +), conditionals 0, loops 1 (reduce), assignments 0. So they didn't count `numbers` property access as invocation. Good.\n\nConditionals: `if`, ternary `?:`, `switch`. We will not count `??`. Loops: `for...of`, `forEach`, `reduce`? In example reduce counted as loop. So reduce/loops count as loops