lain sword expected 105. If first insurance always applies, plain sword would be 115. But tests 2-5 expected 105/65/85/45/30. So first insurance doesn't apply to those tests.\n\nHmm. The tests I wrote didn't include first insurance. The spec examples for single items didn't mention first insurance. The integration example mentions first insurance in the context of a newcomer and a second contract.\n\nWait, the integration example for newcomer: \"customer: 0 years with MHPCO, no previous contract; item: cursed sword; premium: 165; (100 base + 50 curse + 10 first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G)\". So first insurance applied there.\n\nBut for single plain sword test, I expected 105 (no first insurance). This contradicts the \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance\" statement.\n\nRe-reading the integration: \"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nThis suggests first insurance applies to every quote. But then why didn't the single item examples include it?\n\nMaybe the single item examples in the spec are \"base premiums\" isolated, not final premiums. The integration examples are the final premiums. But my tests treated single item examples as final premiums.\n\nHmm. There's ambiguity. Let me re-read the spec carefully.\n\n\"Examples: The examples below illustrate single rules in isolation. Values shown are base premiums or raw payout amounts before integration with other modifiers, unless stated otherwise.\"\n\nSo the single item base premiums (100 for sword, etc.) are base premiums before integration. The integration examples show final premiums with all modifiers.\n\nBut my test list treated single item tests as final premiums without modifiers. That was my interpretation. The TDD test list skill says to turn examples into tests. For \"Sword: 1000 G insurance value, 100 G base premium\", I created a test expecting premium 105 (base + fee). But maybe I should have expected only the base premium? No, the function returns final premium.\n\nThe integration examples clarify how modifiers stack. The first insurance surcharge applies in integration scenarios. For simple tests, we can choose not to apply it, treating them as isolated rule tests. But the spec says examples are before integration unless stated otherwise. The integration examples are stated otherwise.\n\nSo in my test list, I chose to apply only the specific rule being tested. For first insurance test, I should apply only first insurance (and fee). For plain sword, I applied no modifiers except fee.\n\nBut the integration example says each item is treated as first insurance. This creates tension.\n\nMaybe the rule is: first insurance surcharge applies, but for the simple single-item tests, I intentionally didn't include it to test the base premium in isolation. That's a valid testing approach.\n\nFor the first insurance test, I should apply first insurance to a plain sword: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115. That matches what I just activated.\n\nBut wait, if first insurance applies to all quotes, then the previous plain sword test (test 2) should also have first insurance and expect 115, not 105. That means my earlier tests are wrong according to the \"each item is first insurance\" rule.\n\nThis is a design decision. I think the cleanest interpretation for the kata is:\n- The simple rule-isolation tests apply only that rule (plus fee).\n- The integration tests apply all relevant modifiers.\n\nThe integration example explicitly states first insurance applies. So in integration tests, it applies. In isolated tests, we can test modifiers individually.\n\nGiven that I've already written tests 2-6 without first insurance and they pass, I'll continue with the isolated-test approach. The first insurance test will apply only first insurance.\n\nSo in the implementation, first insurance surcharge should apply when... how do we know? If I implement it as always applying, then tests 2-6 would fail. But they don't fail because I haven't implemented first insurance yet.\n\nI need a way to conditionally apply first insurance. But the condition is \"first insurance\" which means the first contract/quote. In my isolated tests, I don't want it applied except in the first-insurance test.\n\nActually, the cleanest way is: for the first-insurance test, the scenario has only one step (a quote), and it's the first quote. So first insurance should apply. But then for test 2 (single sword), it's also a first quote, so first insurance should apply.\n\nThis is the contradiction. Let me think about how the original kata author intended it.\n\nRe-reading the integration examples more carefully:\n\nIntegration 1: \"customer: 0 years with MHPCO, no previous contract\" - explicitly says no previous contract. Premium includes first insurance.\n\nIntegration 2: \"customer: 3 years with MHPCO; this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\" - explicitly says second quote. Premium includes both first insurance and follow-up discount.\n\nThis suggests:\n- First insurance surcharge applies to the first quote in a scenario (or when no previous contract).\n- Follow-up discount applies to subsequent quotes.\n\nSo in a scenario with a single quote, first insurance applies. But my test 2 has a single quote and doesn't include first insurance.\n\nThe resolution: my test 2 is an isolated base-premium test, not a full integration test. It intentionally omits first insurance. This is a testing choice, not necessarily a spec violation.\n\nBut now I'm implementing the actual system. I need to decide whether first insurance applies to all first quotes. If yes, I need to update previous tests. If no, I need to understand when it applies.\n\nGiven the integration examples, I'll implement: first insurance surcharge applies to the first quote step in a scenario. Then I'll need to update tests 2-6 and 8-12 to account for first insurance. But that would change many passing tests.\n\nAlternatively, I can treat first insurance as an opt-in modifier that only applies when there's some indicator. But the spec doesn't mention such an indicator.\n\nHmm. Let me look at the integration example values again:\n- Newcomer with cursed sword: 100 + 50 + 10 = 160 + 5 = 165. Here first insurance is 10% of policy base (100).\n- Long-standing customer's second contract: 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155 + 5 = 160. Here first insurance = 10, follow-up = -15.\n\nSo first insurance applies even in the second contract! The text says \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\" So it's not about first vs second quote. It's about each item being a first insurance.\n\nThis means first insurance surcharge applies to every quote. So my tests 2-6 are wrong if they don't include it.\n\nBut wait, the simple examples (e.g., \"Sword: 1000 G insurance value, 100 G base premium\") are just price list entries, not final premiums. My tests should have included first insurance if the rule applies to every quote.\n\nI think I need to reconsider. Maybe the rule is: every quote gets first insurance surcharge. Then tests 2-6 should expect +10%. Let me recalculate:\n- Sword: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115\n- Amulet: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71\n- Staff: 80 + 8 + 5 = 93\n- Potion: 40 + 4 + 5 = 49\n- Component: 25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 -> rounded to 33? Wait, rounding happens at the end. 25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5, rounded up to 33.\n\nBut I wrote tests expecting 105, 65, 85, 45, 30. These would all need updating.\n\nHowever, the TDD skill says not to change tests once active. But test list can be adjusted? The test list was created by me, so I can fix it if the spec interpretation changes.\n\nActually, the spec is ambiguous, and the integration examples clarify. I should follow the integration examples: first insurance applies to every quote.\n\nBut then my simple tests are wrong. Let me reconsider whether to change them.\n\nGiven the experiment is about TDD discipline, not about getting the perfect interpretation, maybe I should stick with my initial interpretation (isolated tests) and only apply first insurance in the first-insurance test. That keeps the tests passing and the implementation simpler.\n\nBut the integration test later will require first insurance to apply to the newcomer scenario. If I only apply it conditionally (e.g., when there are modifiers), that won't work.\n\nLet me implement first insurance as always applying (to every quote). Then update the base tests. Actually, no — the base tests are already passing. If I implement first insurance always, they will fail. I'd need to update them.\n\nAlternatively, implement first insurance only when explicitly... no, there's no explicit flag.\n\nI think the cleanest path is to implement first insurance as a policy-wide modifier that always applies to quotes. Then update the simple base-premium tests to include it. But that's a lot of test changes.\n\nWait, actually the simple tests are \"should return premium X for a single plain sword -- 100 base + 5 fee\". The description explicitly says 100 base + 5 fee. So according to my test, no first insurance. I can keep this interpretation for isolated tests and have a separate first-insurance test that adds the 10%.\n\nFor the implementation, I can make first insurance apply only in certain scenarios. But what condition?\n\nMaybe the condition is: first insurance applies to the first quote in a scenario, and follow-up discount applies to subsequent quotes. But the integration example 2 explicitly says second quote still has first insurance. So that's not it.\n\nMaybe first insurance always applies, and my simple tests are just wrong. Let me bite the bullet: implement first insurance always, and update tests 2-6, 8-12 to include it.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the integration example 2: \"this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\". If first insurance always applies and follow-up discount applies to quotes after the first, then:\n- First quote: +10% first insurance\n- Second quote: +10% first insurance, -15% follow-up\n\nThat matches integration 2. And integration 1: first quote, +10%.\n\nSo the rule is:\n- Every quote: +10% first insurance\n- Quotes after the first: -15% follow-up discount\n\nThis makes sense. So I need to implement first insurance always, and follow-up discount for quotes after the first.\n\nBut then my simple base tests need updating. Let me update them now to include first insurance. I'll need to recalculate expected premiums.\n\nActually, looking back at the test list I created, I explicitly wrote tests without first insurance. That was my choice. But to be consistent with the spec's integration examples, I should update those tests.\n\nHowever, there's another consideration: the test list skill says \"The test list comes from the kata's specification -- do not add generic 'validate input types' or 'edge cases' tests unless the spec calls for them.\" It doesn't say I can't adjust expected values.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll make a pragmatic choice: implement first insurance always and follow-up discount for non-first quotes. Then update the affected tests.\n\nBut wait, I have 17 tests passing. Changing them will make them fail. I need to update them and re-run green.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The first insurance test (test 18) is currently failing because I haven't implemented it. If I implement first insurance as always applying, tests 2-17 will fail (except maybe loyalty test). Then I'd need to update them all.\n\nThat's a lot of churn. Maybe I should implement first insurance conditionally so only test 18 gets it. But what condition?\n\nLooking at test 18: customer 0 years, single sword. Test 2: customer 0 years, single sword. The only difference is the expected premium. There's no input difference. So I can't conditionally apply based on input.\n\nUnless I add a flag to the scenario, but the spec doesn't have one.\n\nOK, I think the right interpretation is that first insurance always applies. My simple tests were written incorrectly. I need to update them.\n\nBut actually, let me reconsider: maybe the simple tests in the spec are meant to be base premiums only, and the function's premium includes the processing fee but not other policy-wide modifiers. The integration examples show full premium including first insurance.\n\nIn that case, the function would compute base premium + fee for simple cases, and add policy-wide modifiers only in integration scenarios. But how would it know?\n\nThis is genuinely ambiguous. I'll make a decision and move forward:\n\nDecision: Implement first insurance surcharge as applying to every quote step. Update the simple base-premium tests to include it. The first-insurance test becomes redundant or just confirms it.\n\nBut that means I need to update tests 2-6 and 8-17. Let me think about the new expected values:\n\nFor a quote with customer 0 years, single item, no other modifiers:\n- Sword: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115\n- Amulet: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71\n- Staff: 80 + 8 + 5 = 93\n- Potion: 40 + 4 + 5 = 49\n- Rune: 25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 -> rounded up to 33 (rounding in MHPCO's favor means up for premium)\n\nWait, the rounding rule: \"All amounts are rounded to whole G in the MHPCO's favor.\" For premium, that's rounded up. So 32.5 -> 33.\n\nBut then test 6 (single rune) should expect 33, not 30. And test 7 (2 runes): 50 + 5 + 5 = 60? Wait, first insurance is policy-wide on policy base. Policy base for 2 runes = 50. First insurance = 5. Fee = 5. Total = 60.\n\nTest 8 (3 runes block): policy base = 60. First insurance = 6. Fee = 5. Total = 71.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. And my tests didn't account for rounding.\n\nActually, the simple tests might not produce fractions except for components. For main items: sword 100 * 10% = 10, integer. Amulet 60 * 10% = 6, integer. Staff 80 * 10% = 8. Potion 40 * 10% = 4. All integer.\n\nComponents: rune 25 * 10% = 2.5, not integer. So rounding matters.\n\nBut wait, maybe first insurance is not meant to apply to the component-only tests? The spec examples for components don't mention it.\n\nUgh. Let me step back.\n\nI think the original kata intends:\n- Base premium examples are just the base, no modifiers.\n- The integration examples show full calculation with all modifiers.\n- For testing individual rules, you test that rule in isolation.\n\nSo my initial approach is valid: test rules in isolation. First insurance test applies only first insurance (and fee). Base tests apply no policy-wide modifiers.\n\nBut then how does the implementation know when to apply first insurance? It doesn't, unless we use the integration rule \"always apply\". But then isolated tests fail.\n\nThe resolution: in the implementation, always apply first insurance. In the tests, the \"base\" tests are technically wrong but they were written as isolated behavior tests. To make them pass, I could either:\n1. Update tests to include first insurance.\n2. Not implement first insurance always.\n\nOption 2 contradicts the integration example. So option 1 is better.\n\nBut option 1 creates fractional premiums for components. The rounding rule handles that.\n\nLet me proceed with option 1: implement first insurance always. Update tests to match.\n\nActually wait, I just realized: the test descriptions explicitly say what they expect. Test 2: \"100 base + 5 fee\". If I update it to \"100 base + 10 first insurance + 5 fee\", that's fine. But I have many tests to update.\n\nGiven time constraints, maybe I should keep first insurance as isolated test and not implement it globally. Then for integration tests, I'd need to somehow apply it. But I can't conditionally apply without input difference.\n\nHmm. Another option: implement first insurance always, but realize that the previous tests will need updating. I'll update them all at once.\n\nActually, the simplest path that preserves most tests: implement first insurance always. Then update all previously passing tests that don't already include first insurance in their expectation. This includes tests 2-6, 8-12, 14-17.\n\nTest 13 (cursed sword): expected 155. With first insurance: 100 + 50 + 10 + 5 = 165. Need update.\nTest 14 (enchantment sword): 100 + 30 + 10 + 5 = 145. Need update.\nTest 15 (enchantment amulet): 60 + 18 + 6 + 5 = 89. Need update.\nTest 16 (both): 100 + 80 + 10 + 5 = 195. Need update.\nTest 17 (loyalty): 100 - 20 + 10 + 5 = 95. Need update.\n\nThis is a lot. But it's the correct interpretation.\n\nActually, wait. The integration example 1 (newcomer with cursed sword) gives 165. With my formula: 100 + 50 + 10 + 5 = 165. Yes.\n\nIntegration example 2: 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 + 5 = 160. Yes.\n\nSo if I implement first insurance always and follow-up for non-first quotes, integration tests will pass.\n\nLet me do this. I'll implement first insurance always. Then update tests. But the TDD cycle requires one test at a time. If I implement first insurance always, multiple tests fail. I can't fix them all in one cycle.\n\nHowever, the failing test is test 18. I can implement first insurance in a way that only affects test 18. But there's no input difference.\n\nWait, maybe I can add a flag in the test data? The spec doesn't prohibit extra fields. But the schema doesn't include a \"firstInsurance\" flag. Adding one would be inventing API.\n\nAlternatively, I can change test 18 to be an integration-like scenario where first insurance is expected, and implement first insurance always. Then accept that previous tests need updating, and update them as part of subsequent Red/Green cycles.\n\nBut each Red phase activates one test. If after implementing first insurance, test 2 fails, I can activate test 2 (it's already active) and \"fix\" it by updating expectation. But that's not really TDD.\n\nI think the cleanest TDD approach is:\n1. Implement first insurance always (to make test 18 pass).\n2. Recognize that previous tests now fail.\n3. Update those tests' expectations to include first insurance.\n4. Re-run; they should pass.\n\nBut updating tests outside the Red/Green cycle violates TDD discipline somewhat. However, the tests were written with an incomplete interpretation, and the spec clarification requires updating them.\n\nActually, the skill allows updating the test list. And once a test is active, if the expected value is wrong, we can fix it.\n\nLet me implement first insurance always, then update all affected active tests. For tests that are still todo, I'll update their descriptions.\n\nFirst, implement first insurance.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:113","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-37-06_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7/src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;","newText":"const LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;"},{"oldText":"const loyaltyDiscountRateFor = (customer: Customer): number =>\n  customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const input = scenario as ScenarioInput;\n  const items = input.steps[0].items ?? [];\n  const customer = input.customer;\n  const { policyBase, itemSurcharges } = premiumBreakdownForItems(items as Item[]);\n  const loyaltyDiscount = policyBase * loyaltyDiscountRateFor(customer);\n  const premium = policyBase + itemSurcharges + loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD;\n  return { results: [{ premium }] };\n};","newText":"const loyaltyDiscountRateFor = (customer: Customer): number =>\n  customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n\nconst firstInsuranceSurchargeFor = (_customer: Customer): number => FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const input = scenario as ScenarioInput;\n  const items = input.steps[0].items ?? [];\n  const customer = input.customer;\n  const { policyBase, itemSurcharges } = premiumBreakdownForItems(items as Item[]);\n  const loyaltyDiscount = policyBase * loyaltyDiscountRateFor(customer);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = policyBase * firstInsuranceSurchargeFor(customer);\n  const premium = policyBase + itemSurcharges + loyaltyDiscount + firstInsuranceSurcharge + PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD;\n  return { results: [{ premium }] };\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":628,"output":4937,"cacheRead":69504,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4371,"totalTokens":75069,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784923800927,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-b62bbc61-2869-43f6-b8df-7af966f1e32a","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.edit:113","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 2 block(s) in /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-37-06_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7/src/claim-office.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n  4 const BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD = 60;\n  5 const COMPONENT_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD = 25;\n  6 const LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\n  7 const LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;\n+ 8 const FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\n  8 \n  9 const BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD: Record<string, number> = {\n 10   sword: 100,\n 11   amulet: 60,\n    ...\n 82 \n 83 const loyaltyDiscountRateFor = (customer: Customer): number =>\n 84   customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n 85 \n+87 const firstInsuranceSurchargeFor = (_cus