(the keys are property names, but they're string constants). Numeric values: 100, 60, 80, 40 = 4. Total BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD constants = 8.\n- String literals in INSURANCE_VALUES_GOLD: \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\", \"potion\", \"rune\", \"moonstone\" = 6. Numeric values: 1000, 600, 800, 400, 250, 250 = 6. Total = 12.\n- Set literals in COMPONENT_TYPES: \"rune\", \"moonstone\" = 2.\n- HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD: 5 = 1\n- HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE: 0.3 = 1\n- CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE: 0.5 = 1\n- \"\" in `item.type ?? \"\"` = 1 string constant appearing twice? Actually there are multiple instances. Let me count them when I go through function by function.\n\nActually, let me be more systematic. I'll count constants, bindings, invocations, conditionals, loops, assignments per function.\n\nFunction: `basePremiumForItem`\n```ts\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number =>\n  BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD[item.type ?? \"\"] ?? UNKNOWN_ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD;\n```\n- Constants: \"\" (1)\n- Bindings: item (1)\n- Invocations: BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD access (1), ?? fallback (1) - actually property access is not an invocation. ?? is an operator. Hmm, is `??` an invocation? No, it's a conditional-ish operator. Let me think. The APP categories: constants, bindings, invocations, conditionals, loops, assignments.\n  - `BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD[item.type ?? \"\"]` - property access. Not an invocation.\n  - `item.type ?? \"\"` - nullish coalescing. This is a conditional (ternary-like).\n  - `BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD[...] ?? UNKNOWN_ITEM_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD` - another nullish coalescing, another conditional.\n- Conditionals: 2 (the two ?? operators)\n- Total: constants 1, bindings 1, conditionals 2. Mass = 1 + 1 + 8 = 10.\n\nFunction: `itemSurchargeFor`\n```ts\nconst itemSurchargeFor = (item: Item, basePremium: number): number => {\n  const curseRate = item.cursed ? CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantmentRate =\n    (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n      ? HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n      : 0;\n  return basePremium * (curseRate + enchantmentRate);\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0 (3 occurrences - in curseRate ternary, enchantmentRate ternary, and enchantment ?? 0), HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD (5), HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE (0.3), CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE (0.5). So 6 constants.\n- Bindings: item, basePremium, curseRate, enchantmentRate = 4\n- Invocations: item.enchantment access? Not really. basePremium * (curseRate + enchantmentRate) has * and + operations but those are operators, not invocations.\n- Conditionals: item.cursed ? ... : ... (1), (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= ... ? ... : ... (1). The ?? inside is also a conditional (1). So 3 conditionals.\n- Total: 6 + 4 + 0 + 12 = 22.\n\nFunction: `componentBasePremiumForCount`\n```ts\nconst componentBasePremiumForCount = (count: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK\n    ? BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD\n    : count * COMPONENT_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD;\n```\n- Constants: COMPONENTS_PER_BLOCK (3), BLOCK_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD (60), COMPONENT_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD (25). 3 constants.\n- Bindings: count. 1\n- Invocations: count * COMPONENT_BASE_PREMIUM_GOLD has multiplication, not invocation. \n- Conditionals: 1 ternary.\n- Total: 3 + 1 + 4 = 8.\n\nFunction: `premiumBreakdownForItems`\n```ts\nconst premiumBreakdownForItems = (items: Item[]): PremiumBreakdown => {\n  const componentCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const type = item.type ?? \"\";\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n      componentCounts.set(type, (componentCounts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n    } else {\n      const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n      policyBase += base;\n      itemSurcharges += itemSurchargeFor(item, base);\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (const count of componentCounts.values()) {\n    policyBase += componentBasePremiumForCount(count);\n  }\n\n  return { policyBase, itemSurcharges };\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0 (initial policyBase, initial itemSurcharges, in componentCounts.get ?? 0), \"\" (1). So 3.\n- Bindings: items, componentCounts, policyBase, itemSurcharges, item, type, base, count = 8\n- Invocations: new Map (1), Map<string,number>() generic - not invocation. COMPONENT_TYPES.has (1), componentCounts.set (1), componentCounts.get (1), basePremiumForItem (1), itemSurchargeFor (1), componentBasePremiumForCount (1). So 7 invocations.\n- Conditionals: item.type ?? \"\" (1), if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) (1), componentCounts.get(type) ?? 0 (1). So 3 conditionals.\n- Loops: 2 for...of loops = 2\n- Assignments: componentCounts.set (1), policyBase += base (1), itemSurcharges += itemSurchargeFor(item, base) (1), policyBase += componentBasePremiumForCount(count) (1). So 4 assignments. But also const declarations? No, assignments are mutations. += counts as assignment. set() is method call with side effect, not assignment operator.\n- Total: 3 + 8 + 14 + 12 + 10 + 24 = 71.\n\nFunction: `loyaltyDiscountRateFor`\n```ts\nconst loyaltyDiscountRateFor = (customer: Customer): number =>\n  customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? -LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n```\n- Constants: LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD (2), LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE (0.2), 0. 3 constants.\n- Bindings: customer. 1\n- Conditionals: 1\n- Total: 3 + 1 + 4 = 8.\n\nFunction: `followUpDiscountRateFor`\n```ts\nconst followUpDiscountRateFor = (quoteIndex: number): number =>\n  quoteIndex > 0 ? -FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n```\n- Constants: 0, FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE (0.15). 2 constants.\n- Bindings: quoteIndex. 1\n- Conditionals: 1\n- Total: 2 + 1 + 4 = 7.\n\nFunction: `quotePremium`\n```ts\nconst quotePremium = (customer: Customer, items: Item[], quoteIndex: number): QuoteResult => {\n  const { policyBase, itemSurcharges } = premiumBreakdownForItems(items);\n  const policyWideRate =\n    loyaltyDiscountRateFor(customer) +\n    FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE +\n    followUpDiscountRateFor(quoteIndex);\n  const premium = policyBase + itemSurcharges + policyBase * policyWideRate + PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD;\n  return { premium: Math.ceil(premium) };\n};\n```\n- Constants: FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE (0.1), PROCESSING_FEE_GOLD (5). 2 constants.\n- Bindings: customer, items, quoteIndex, policyBase, itemSurcharges, policyWideRate, premium = 7\n- Invocations: premiumBreakdownForItems (1), loyaltyDiscountRateFor (1), followUpDiscountRateFor (1), Math.ceil (1). So 4.\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Total: 2 + 7 + 8 = 17.\n\nFunction: `createPolicy`\n```ts\nconst createPolicy = (items: Item[]): Policy => {\n  const insuranceSum = items.reduce(\n    (sum, item) => sum + (INSURANCE_VALUES_GOLD[item.type ?? \"\"] ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n  return { insuranceSum, remainingCap: insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER, items };\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0 (initial value), \"\" (1), 0 (fallback). 3 constants.\n- Bindings: items, insuranceSum, sum, item. 4\n- Invocations: items.reduce (1)\n- Conditionals: item.type ?? \"\" (1), INSURANCE_VALUES_GOLD[...] ?? 0 (1). 2 conditionals.\n- Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration)\n- Total: 3 + 4 + 2 + 8 + 5 = 22.\n\nFunction: `reimbursementFor`\n```ts\nconst reimbursementFor = (damage: { itemType: string; amount: number }, _policy: Policy): number => {\n  return Math.max(0, damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD);\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0, DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD (100). 2 constants.\n- Bindings: damage, _policy. 2\n- Invocations: Math.max (1)\n- Conditionals: 0 (Math.max has internal comparison but at call level we just count the invocation)\n- Total: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\n\nFunction: `processClaim`\n```ts\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: { damages: Array<{ itemType: string; amount: number }> }): ClaimResult => {\n  let totalPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    totalPayout += reimbursementFor(damage, policy);\n  }\n  const payout = Math.min(totalPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  const roundedPayout = Math.floor(payout);\n  return { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - roundedPayout };\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0. 1 constant.\n- Bindings: policy, incident, totalPayout, damage, payout, roundedPayout. 6\n- Invocations: reimbursementFor (1), Math.min (1), Math.floor (1). 3\n- Loops: 1\n- Assignments: totalPayout += reimbursementFor(damage, policy) (1). 1\n- Total: 1 + 6 + 6 + 5 + 6 = 24.\n\nFunction: `processScenario`\n```ts\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): { results: Result[] } => {\n  const input = scenario as ScenarioInput;\n  const results: Result[] = [];\n  const policies = new Map<number, Policy>();\n  let quoteIndex = 0;\n\n  for (let stepIndex = 0; stepIndex < input.steps.length; stepIndex += 1) {\n    const step = input.steps[stepIndex];\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const items = step.items ?? [];\n      const policy = createPolicy(items as Item[]);\n      policies.set(stepIndex, policy);\n      results.push(quotePremium(input.customer, items as Item[], quoteIndex));\n      quoteIndex += 1;\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      const policy = policies.get(step.policy ?? -1);\n      if (!policy || !step.incident) {\n        results.push({ payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 });\n      } else {\n        results.push(processClaim(policy, step.incident));\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return { results };\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0 (initial quoteIndex), 0 (stepIndex initial), 1 (stepIndex += 1), \"quote\", \"claim\", -1, 0 (payout), 0 (remainingCap). So 8 constants. Also the object keys \"results\" are property names, not values.\n- Bindings: scenario, input, results, policies, quoteIndex, stepIndex, step, items, policy (quote branch), policy (claim branch). That's 10. Also damage? No, not in this function.\n- Invocations: input.steps.length access? Not invocation. policies.set (1), createPolicy (1), results.push (1) in quote branch, quotePremium (1). In claim branch: policies.get (1), results.push (2, one in each sub-branch), processClaim (1). So total: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 8.\n- Conditionals: step.op === \"quote\" (1), step.op === \"claim\" (1), step.items ?? [] (1), step.policy ?? -1 (1), !policy || !step.incident (2 conditionals - the || is two). So 6 conditionals.\n- Loops: 1 for loop\n- Assignments: quoteIndex += 1 (1), policies.set (1), results.push (1), results.push (1). Actually results.push is method call with side effect, not assignment operator. But += and ++ are assignments. stepIndex += 1 (1), quoteIndex += 1 (1). So 2 assignments. policies.set has side effect but is invocation. results.push is invocation.\n- Total: 8 + 10 + 16 + 24 + 5 + 12 = 75.\n\nOther code mass:\n- Constants declarations at top: 11 numeric + the object literals. Let me not count declarations since they're global constants. Actually we should count the expressions. But constants declared with `const` are bindings, not constants in the APP sense. APP constants are literal values. The declaration itself creates a binding.\n\nThis is getting complicated. For the purpose of this task, I'll calculate mass for the functions I modify.\n\nInitial mass summary for functions I'll modify:\n- processScenario: ~75\n- reimbursementFor: 6\n- processClaim: 24\n\nAfter extracting quote/claim step processors, processScenario mass will decrease but total mass may increase slightly due to new function overhead. However, clarity (Rule 2) is more important than mass.\n\nLet me focus on meaningful refactorings that improve clarity.\n\nRefactoring plan:\n1. Rename `_policy` to `policy` in `reimbursementFor` - removes naming smell.\n2. Extract `processQuoteStep` and `processClaimStep` from `processScenario` - improves intent.\n3. Add explaining variable or extract `policyWideRate` calculation? Already done.\n\nActually, looking at `processScenario`, the dispatch could be clearer with a `switch` or separate handlers. I'll extract the step processing functions.\n\nLet me also consider:\n- The `Step` type uses `items?: unknown[]` which forces `as Item[]` casts. This is a type smell. Could we change the type to `Item[]`? But the scenario input is `unknown`, so maybe the casting is necessary. We could improve by adding a type guard or parsing function. But that might be over-engineering.\n\nI think the best refactorings are:\n1. Rename `_policy` to `policy`.\n2. Extract step handlers from `processScenario`.\n\nLet me implement these one at a time.\n\nFirst, let me do the rename.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Tests pass. Now I'll evaluate naming and calculate APP mass before refactoring.\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- `processScenario` — clearly orchestrates scenario processing.\n- `createPolicy`, `quotePremium`, `processClaim` — all descriptive.\n- `reimbursementFor` — descriptive, but parameter is named `_policy`. The underscore prefix signals “unused,” yet it’s part of the public helper shape. This is a naming smell; future claim rules will likely need the policy. Rename to `policy`.\n\n**Initial APP Mass** (functions to be modified):\n- `processScenario`: ~75\n- `processClaim`: 24\n- `reimbursementFor`: 6\n\nFirst improvement: remove the `_policy` naming smell in `reimbursementFor`."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:3","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const reimbursementFor = (damage: { itemType: string; amount: number }, _policy: Policy): number => {","newText":"const reimbursementFor = (damage: { itemType: string; amount: number }, policy: Policy): number => {"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":245,"output":4013,"cacheRead":11712,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":3769,"totalTokens":15970,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784924100991,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-7f9d4d26-1de1-4268-a525-ddf1d3a06959","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.edit:3","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully rep