n't specify what the second parameter is. It could be renamed to `surchargeForItem(item, basePremium)` or `calculateItemSurcharge(item, basePremium)`.\n\nActually, looking more carefully, the naming is mostly clear. But `itemSurchargeFor` could be improved.\n\n## Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n\nIssues:\n1. `reimbursementFor` has an empty `else if` branch for dragon material:\n```typescript\n} else if (material === DRAGON_MATERIAL) {\n  // full reimbursement, amount unchanged\n}\n```\nThis is dead code / misleading. It doesn't do anything but adds a conditional. The comment explains why, but the code could be clearer.\n\nWait, but the tests show that dragon material gives full reimbursement. The current code gives full reimbursement by default (no condition). The empty branch is conceptual duplication of \"default behavior\". It adds mass without value. But it might be there to express the business rule. However, a comment would be clearer, or we could invert the logic.\n\nActually, the dragon material rule is \"full reimbursement\". Since default is full reimbursement, we don't need a special branch. But the test list shows there are tests for dragon material. The current code handles high enchantment first (which reduces reimbursement), and everything else gets full reimbursement. So the dragon branch is unnecessary.\n\nBut wait - is there a risk that future tests might add more rules? The code is already written to handle the existing tests. We should refactor to improve clarity.\n\nThe empty `else if` branch is confusing because:\n1. It suggests dragon material needs special handling\n2. It adds a conditional with no effect\n3. It could be replaced with a comment or removed\n\nHowever, if we remove it, we lose the explicit mention of the dragon material rule. A better approach might be to make the logic clearer with explaining variables or comments.\n\nActually, looking at it more: the logic is:\n- If enchantment >= 8: 50% reimbursement\n- Else if dragon material: full reimbursement (which is the default)\n- Else: full reimbursement\n\nSo the dragon branch is redundant. We could remove it and add a comment explaining that dragon material has no special reduction. This would reduce mass and improve clarity.\n\nBut wait, this might be intentional to document the rule. Let me think about Rule 2 vs Rule 4. Rule 2 says reveal intent. The empty branch with comment does reveal intent but in a clumsy way. A better way to reveal intent might be:\n\n```typescript\nconst isHighEnchantment = enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD;\nconst isDragonMaterial = material === DRAGON_MATERIAL;\n\nif (isHighEnchantment) {\n  amount *= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n} else if (!isDragonMaterial) {\n  // Only high-enchantment and dragon-material items have special reimbursement rules.\n  // Dragon material is fully reimbursed, high enchantment is halved.\n}\n```\n\nThat doesn't make sense either.\n\nActually, the cleanest refactor would be:\n\n```typescript\nconst reimbursementFor = (damage: Damage, policy: Policy): number => {\n  const item = policy.items.find((policyItem) => policyItem.type === damage.itemType);\n  const enchantment = item?.enchantment ?? 0;\n  \n  let amount = damage.amount;\n  if (enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD) {\n    amount *= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n  }\n  // Dragon material and non-special items are reimbursed at full damage amount.\n  \n  return Math.max(0, amount - DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD);\n};\n```\n\nBut that removes the explicit mention of dragon material. Given there are tests specifically for dragon material, maybe it's better to keep it explicit but in a clearer way.\n\nAlternative:\n\n```typescript\nconst applyReimbursementModifier = (item: Item | undefined, amount: number): number => {\n  const enchantment = item?.enchantment ?? 0;\n  if (enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD) {\n    return amount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n  }\n  return amount;\n};\n```\n\nThen in `reimbursementFor`:\n```typescript\nconst reimbursementFor = (damage: Damage, policy: Policy): number => {\n  const item = policy.items.find((policyItem) => policyItem.type === damage.itemType);\n  const adjustedAmount = applyReimbursementModifier(item, damage.amount);\n  return Math.max(0, adjustedAmount - DEDUCTIBLE_GOLD);\n};\n```\n\nThis removes the dragon branch entirely because it's the default behavior. This is cleaner.\n\nBut I need to consider: does removing the dragon branch reduce clarity? The tests verify dragon material behavior. If someone reads the code, they might wonder why dragon material isn't mentioned. A comment could help. But the current empty branch is also confusing.\n\nActually, I think extracting `applyReimbursementModifier` is a good refactor. It:\n1. Removes the empty/misleading dragon branch\n2. Makes the function name reveal intent\n3. Reduces duplication (the high-enchantment check is now in one place)\n4. Makes the code easier to test\n\nLet me also check for other refactoring opportunities.\n\n## Rule 3: No Duplication\n\nLooking for duplication:\n- `basePremiumForItem` uses `item.type ?? \"\"` - this pattern is repeated in `createPolicy` and `processScenario` loop\n- The `item.type ?? \"\"` fallback appears multiple times\n- `INSURANCE_VALUES_GOLD[item.type ?? \"\"] ?? 0` in `createPolicy`\n\nCould extract a helper to get item type, but `?? \"\"` is simple enough.\n\nIn `premiumBreakdownForItems`:\n```typescript\nconst type = item.type ?? \"\";\nif (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n  componentCounts.set(type, (componentCounts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n}\n```\nThe `?? \"\"` is used once here. Not much duplication.\n\nIn `processScenario`:\n```typescript\nfor (let stepIndex = 0; stepIndex < scenario.steps.length; stepIndex += 1) {\n  const step = scenario.steps[stepIndex];\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n```\nThis is fine.\n\n## Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n\nThe `material` variable in `reimbursementFor` is only used in the dragon br