elper `itemType(item)` to remove repeated `item.type ?? \"\"`. This reduces duplication and improves clarity. But might not reduce APP much? It adds function invocation, but removes repeated conditional coalescing? `??` is not a conditional in APP? The instructions list conditionals as `if`, `switch`, `?:`. `??` is a binary operator, not conditional? It's a short-circuit operator. Probably not counted as conditional. But bindings/invocations etc. We need define APP mass counting precisely. We can calculate before and after.\n\n2. Improve naming: `reimbursableDamageFor` -> `reimbursableAmountFor`. `validateQuoteItems` -> `assertAllItemTypesAreKnown`. Maybe rename `basePremiumForItem` etc.\n\n3. Remove unused `itemCountsByType` and `damageCountsByType` wrappers? They add fewest elements. But they reveal intent. CountByKey generic plus wrappers might be considered duplication? Could inline countByKey logic into wrappers to remove generic function (fewer elements). But generic function reused. Hmm.\n\n4. Merge `loyaltyDiscountRateFor` and `followUpDiscountRateFor` into `policyWideAdjustmentRateFor`? But they have distinct semantics.\n\n5. The `processClaimStep` currently returns zero if policy missing or incident missing. The tests may rely on that for unknown types? Actually there is no test for missing incident. Fine.\n\n6. Potential bug / design: `validateQuoteItems` throws for unknown types in createPolicy. This is what was added. It uses `KNOWN_ITEM_TYPES` built from base premiums and component types. Good.\n\nRefactor must keep tests green. Need one change. Could be extracting `itemType` helper and replacing repeated `item.type ?? \"\"`. That is a safe improvement. Need recalc APP mass.\n\nBut we must be careful: TypeScript `item.type ?? \"\"` has nullish coalescing. If item.type could be undefined, the helper returns string. Many uses:\n- basePremiumForItem: `BASE_PREMIUMS_GOLD[item.type ?? \"\"]`\n- premiumBreakdownForItems: `const type = item.type ?? \"\"; if C