tions; should be consistent.\n\nWe can simplify: count bindings, invocations, conditionals, loops, assignments, constants (numeric/string/boolean/null/undefined? and maybe template literals?). We can present counts with caveat. But need before and after. Need a concrete improvement. We can calculate difference.\n\nPotential refactor with clearer impact on mass: Replace imperative loops with reduce? That might increase mass. Replace repeated `item.type ?? \"\"` with helper reduces repeated `??` operations but adds function invocations and a binding. Need decide.\n\nMaybe a more impactful refactor: Simplify `premiumBreakdownForItems` by using `countByKey` for component counts as well, and then process component counts. This reduces duplication of counting logic and the local `componentCounts` map building loop. It uses generic helper `countByKey`, which is already present. That would remove one loop and local counting logic. It might reduce mass (removing loop, assignments). Let's explore.\n\nCurrent `premiumBreakdownForItems` loops over items:\n```\nconst componentCounts = new Map<string, number>();\nlet policyBase = 0;\nlet itemSurcharges = 0;\n\nfor (const item of items) {\n    const type = item.type ?? \"\";\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)) {\n      componentCounts.set(type, (componentCounts.get(type) ?? 0) + 1);\n    } else {\n      const base = basePremiumForItem(item);\n      policyBase += base;\n      itemSurcharges += itemSurchargeFor(item, base);\n    }\n  }\n\nfor (const count of componentCounts.values()) {\n    policyBase += componentBasePremiumForCount(count);\n  }\n```\nThis duplicates counting pattern already in countByKey. We could filter component items, then count them using countByKey:\n```\nconst componentItems = items.filter(item => COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type ?? \"\"));\nconst componentCounts = countByKey(componentItems, item => item.type ?? \"\");\nconst nonComponentItems = items.filter(item => !COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type ?? \"\"));\n```